I have gathered a complete picture. Here is my thorough report.

## Overview

This is a Spring PetClinic fork with an OfficeFloor "arm": owner creation is a `POST /api/owners` pipeline of small single-purpose function classes, wired together in a YAML orchestration file. All owner-creation functions live in one package:

`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/`

Supporting domain/lookup helpers live in:

`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/model/`

The pipeline order is defined in `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/resources/officefloor/rest/api/owners.POST.yml`. The relevant slice of that flow is:

```
build -> assignHousehold -> ensureUniqueIdentity -> countHouseholdMembers
  -> applyRegistrationDate -> assignCustomerCode -> assignMembershipNumber
  -> countNamesakes -> assignMembershipLevel -> save -> audit
  -> resolveBulkWarning -> respond
```

So `customerCode` is assigned first, then `membershipNumber` (which reads `customerCode` and `registrationDate`), and `audit` runs after `save`.

Important: the header of `AcceptanceTest.java` states the redesign target — `customerCode` must become `<REGION>-<HASH8>` (currently it is `<CITY3>-<LAST3>-<NNNN>`).

---

## 1. customerCode construction (sequence numbers, region, hashing)

### `AssignCustomerCode.java`
`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/AssignCustomerCode.java`

Current format is `<CITY3>-<LAST3>-<NNNN>` (e.g. `LON-SMI-0007`). Sequence = per-city owner count + 1. No hashing, no region in the code today.

```java
public class AssignCustomerCode {

    public void service(@Val Owner owner, OwnerRepository ownerRepository) {
        String city3 = prefix(owner.getCity());
        String last3 = prefix(owner.getLastName());
        long sequence = ownerRepository.countByCity(owner.getCity()) + 1;
        owner.setCustomerCode(String.format("%s-%s-%04d", city3, last3, sequence));
    }

    /** Upper-cased first three letters of {@code value} (fewer if it is shorter). */
    private static String prefix(String value) {
        return value.substring(0, Math.min(3, value.length())).toUpperCase(Locale.ROOT);
    }
}
```

The sequence source is `OwnerRepository.countByCity(String city)` (see below). The field is stored on the entity via `owner.setCustomerCode(...)` (column `customer_code`).

### Region derivation (from postcode, then city) — used for `locality` today, and is the intended source of `<REGION>` for the redesign

`CityRegion.java` — `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/model/CityRegion.java`:
```java
public static final String UNKNOWN = "UNKNOWN";
private static final Map<String, String> CITY_TO_REGION = Map.of(
    "Sydney", "NSW", "Melbourne", "VIC", "Brisbane", "QLD");
public static String of(String city) {
    return CITY_TO_REGION.getOrDefault(city, UNKNOWN);
}
```

`RegionPostcodes.java` — `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/model/RegionPostcodes.java`:
```java
private static final Map<String, int[]> REGION_TO_RANGE = Map.of(
    "NSW", new int[] {2000, 2099},
    "VIC", new int[] {3000, 3099},
    "QLD", new int[] {4000, 4099});

public static boolean isInRange(String region, int postcode) {
    int[] range = REGION_TO_RANGE.get(region);
    return range == null || (postcode >= range[0] && postcode <= range[1]);
}

public static String regionOf(String postcode) {
    if (postcode == null || !postcode.matches("[0-9]{4}")) {
        return null;
    }
    int value = Integer.parseInt(postcode);
    for (Map.Entry<String, int[]> entry : REGION_TO_RANGE.entrySet()) {
        int[] range = entry.getValue();
        if (value >= range[0] && value <= range[1]) {
            return entry.getKey();
        }
    }
    return null;
}
```

### Existing hashing pattern (for `<HASH8>` reference) — `Household.java`
`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/Household.java`

Not part of customerCode today, but this is the only existing SHA-256 hex-formatting code in the owner package (id form `H-<hex>`, 6 bytes -> 12 upper hex chars). Relevant for the redesign's `<HASH8>`:
```java
static String idFor(String lastName, String address) {
    byte[] digest = sha256(canonical(lastName) + '|' + AddressNormalizer.normalize(address));
    StringBuilder id = new StringBuilder("H-");
    for (int i = 0; i < 6; i++) {
        id.append(String.format("%02X", digest[i]));
    }
    return id.toString();
}
private static byte[] sha256(String value) {
    try {
        return MessageDigest.getInstance("SHA-256").digest(value.getBytes(StandardCharsets.UTF_8));
    } catch (NoSuchAlgorithmException e) {
        throw new IllegalStateException("SHA-256 unavailable", e);
    }
}
```

### Sequence-number source — `OwnerRepository.java`
`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/repository/OwnerRepository.java`
```java
/** ... Used to build the per-city sequence embedded in an owner's {@code customerCode}. */
long countByCity(String city) throws DataAccessException;
```
This is implemented by the JDBC/JPA/Spring-Data owner repositories (`repository/jdbc/JdbcOwnerRepositoryImpl.java`, `repository/jpa/JpaOwnerRepositoryImpl.java`, `repository/springdatajpa/SpringDataOwnerRepository.java`).

---

## 2. Membership number and check digit

### `AssignMembershipNumber.java`
`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/AssignMembershipNumber.java`

Format `<customerCode>-M<YY>`, YY = last two digits of registration-date year. Runs after `AssignCustomerCode` and `ApplyRegistrationDate`.
```java
public void service(@Val Owner owner) {
    int yy = owner.getRegistrationDate().getYear() % 100;
    owner.setMembershipNumber(String.format("%s-M%02d", owner.getCustomerCode(), yy));
}
```

### Check digit — `Luhn.java` + `Owner.getCheckDigit()`
`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/model/Luhn.java`:
```java
public static int checkDigit(String value) {
    if (value == null) {
        return 0;
    }
    int sum = 0;
    boolean doubling = true;
    for (int i = value.length() - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
        char c = value.charAt(i);
        if (c < '0' || c > '9') {
            continue;
        }
        int digit = c - '0';
        if (doubling) {
            digit *= 2;
            if (digit > 9) {
                digit -= 9;
            }
        }
        sum += digit;
        doubling = !doubling;
    }
    return (10 - (sum % 10)) % 10;
}
```

The check digit is a transient derived getter on the entity (`Owner.java`, lines 189-196), computed over the `customerCode`:
```java
@Transient
public int getCheckDigit() {
    return Luhn.checkDigit(this.customerCode);
}
```

### Registration date that feeds the `-M<YY>` segment
- `ResolveRegistrationDate.java` — resolves supplied-or-today, rolled to a business day, published as an OfficeFloor `Out<LocalDate>`.
- `BusinessDay.rollForward(LocalDate)` — weekend rolls to next Monday.
- `ApplyRegistrationDate.java` — sets it on the owner before `AssignMembershipNumber`.

---

## 3. Create audit record logic

### `AuditOwnerCreated.java`
`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/AuditOwnerCreated.java`

Emits a single line to a dedicated SLF4J logger named `AUDIT`, after `SaveOwner` (so `id` exists):
```java
public class AuditOwnerCreated {

    private static final Logger AUDIT = LoggerFactory.getLogger("AUDIT");

    public void service(@Val Owner owner) {
        AUDIT.info("Owner created: id={} customerCode={} registrationDate={} membershipLevel={}",
                owner.getId(), owner.getCustomerCode(), owner.getRegistrationDate(),
                owner.getMembershipLevel());
    }
}
```
Wired as the `audit` step in `owners.POST.yml` (after `save`, before `resolveBulkWarning`). Tests capture this logger via `AuditLogCapture` (see section 6).

---

## 4. Locality derivation

Locality is a transient derived getter on the entity — `Owner.getLocality()` (`Owner.java` lines 177-187). It prefers the postcode-derived region, falling back to the city table:
```java
@Transient
public String getLocality() {
    String byPostcode = RegionPostcodes.regionOf(this.postcode);
    return byPostcode != null ? byPostcode : CityRegion.of(this.city);
}
```
Backed by `RegionPostcodes.regionOf` and `CityRegion.of` (quoted in section 1).

Note the discrepancy to flag for the redesign: the OpenAPI doc for `locality` (`openapi.yml` line 2100) still describes it as city-derived only ("the canonical region derived from the city ... or 'UNKNOWN'"), while the code already does postcode-first. Postcode validation that also uses these tables is in `ValidateOwnerPostcode.java` (rejects a present 4-digit postcode outside its city's region range).

---

## 5. Telephone normalization

### `NormalizeOwnerTelephone.java` (pipeline step `normalizeTelephone`)
`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/NormalizeOwnerTelephone.java`
```java
public void service(@Val OwnerFieldsDto request) throws InvalidTelephoneException {
    String e164 = E164Telephone.format(request.getTelephone());
    if (e164 == null) {
        throw new InvalidTelephoneException(request.getTelephone());
    }
    request.setTelephone(e164);
}
```

### `E164Telephone.java` (the actual algorithm)
`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/E164Telephone.java`

Key logic: strips spaces/dashes/brackets; keeps leading `+`+country code, else assumes `+61` and drops a single leading `0`; requires 8-15 digits after `+`; enforces per-country national lengths (`+61`->9, `+1`->10).
```java
private static final String DEFAULT_COUNTRY_CODE = "61";
private static final Pattern SEPARATORS = Pattern.compile("[\\s()\\-]");
private static final Pattern E164_DIGITS = Pattern.compile("\\d{8,15}");
private static final Map<String, Integer> NATIONAL_LENGTHS = Map.of("61", 9, "1", 10);

static String format(String raw) {
    if (raw == null) { return null; }
    String cleaned = SEPARATORS.matcher(raw).replaceAll("");
    String digits;
    if (cleaned.startsWith("+")) {
        digits = cleaned.substring(1);
    } else {
        String national = cleaned.startsWith("0") ? cleaned.substring(1) : cleaned;
        digits = DEFAULT_COUNTRY_CODE + national;
    }
    if (!E164_DIGITS.matcher(digits).matches() || !hasValidNationalLength(digits)) {
        return null;
    }
    return "+" + digits;
}
```
(`hasValidNationalLength` picks the longest matching country-code prefix and enforces its fixed national length.)

Related: `InvalidTelephoneException.java` and `InvalidTelephoneExceptionHandler.java` (responds 400 with `{"errors":["telephone"]}`); escalation wiring `officefloor/escalation/org.springframework.samples.petclinic.rest.escalation.InvalidTelephoneException.yml`. The normalized telephone is also part of `Owner.getIdentityKey()` (used by `EnsureUniqueIdentity`), and the entity has a `@Pattern(regexp = "^\\+[0-9]{8,15}$")` on `telephone`.

---

## 6. Existing tests

The test tree is small (black-box acceptance only), under `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/test/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/acceptance/`:

### `AcceptanceTest.java`
The only concrete customerCode test — and it encodes the redesign target, not the current behavior:
```java
/** global-id: Redesign owner identity. The customerCode must become '<REGION>-<HASH8>' where REGION is t... */
@Test
void coreCustomerCodeIsRegionHash() throws Exception {
    int id = createOwnerOk(withPostcode(ownerNode()));
    JsonNode n = fetchOwner(id);
    assertTrue(n.get("customerCode").asText().matches("[A-Z0-9]+-[0-9A-F]{8}")); // <REGION>-<HASH8>
}
```

### `AcceptanceBase.java`
Provides the reusable harness and — importantly — reference implementations of the exact algorithms the redesign will need, so tests can assert precise derived values:
- `CITY_REGION`, `REGION_TIMEZONE`, `REGION_POSTCODES` pinned tables (lines 228-237), and `region(city)` / `validPostcode(city)` helpers.
- `sha256hex(String)` and `shaHex(String s, int n)` — "first n UPPER-case hex chars of SHA-256" (lines 282-299) — the reference for `<HASH8>`.
- `luhn(String)` — reference Luhn check digit (lines 302-321).
- Payload builders `ownerNode()`, `withPostcode(...)`, `knownOwner(city)`, `structuredOwner()`; request helpers `createOwnerOk`, `fetchOwner`, `fillCity`, `createMany`.
- Class-level notes reference the derived rules: "membership number, per-day cap, per-city cap, 'most common city'", locality "NSW" for Sydney, and audit assertions via `@WithMockUser(username = "acceptance-admin", ...)`.

### `AuditLogCapture.java`
Test utility that attaches a Logback `ListAppender` to the `AUDIT` logger so the create-audit side effect can be asserted: `messages()`, `anyContains(...)`, `anyAtLevel(level, ...)`, `count()`. This is how `AuditOwnerCreated` is verified.

There are currently no dedicated unit tests for `Luhn`, `CityRegion`, `RegionPostcodes`, `E164Telephone`, or the individual functions — coverage is entirely black-box through the acceptance base.

---

## Package / directory layout for owner-related code

Owner-creation functions (one class per pipeline step):
`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/`
- Identity/derivation: `AssignCustomerCode`, `AssignMembershipNumber`, `AssignMembershipLevel`, `AssignHousehold`, `Household`, `CountNamesakes`, `CountHouseholdMembers`, `EnsureUniqueIdentity`
- Normalization/validation: `NormalizeOwnerTelephone`, `E164Telephone`, `NormalizeOwnerEmail`, `NormalizeOwnerAddress`, `AddressNormalizer`, `ValidateOwnerPostcode`, `ValidateRequiredOwnerFields`, `ValidateOwner`
- Dates: `ResolveRegistrationDate`, `BusinessDay`, `ApplyRegistrationDate`
- Build/persist/respond: `BuildOwner`, `ApplyOwner`, `SaveOwner`, `AuditOwnerCreated`, `RespondWithOwnerCreated`, plus capacity/limit guards (`EnsureCityCapacity`, `EnsureDailyOwnerLimit`, `ResolveBulkSignupWarning`)

Domain models / lookup tables:
`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/model/`
- `Owner.java` (fields `customerCode`, `membershipNumber`, `householdId`, `namesakeCount`, `membershipLevel`; transient getters `getLocality`, `getCheckDigit`, `getContactPreference`, `getIdentityKey`)
- `Luhn.java`, `CityRegion.java`, `RegionPostcodes.java`

Escalations: `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/escalation/` (`InvalidTelephoneException`, `InvalidTelephoneExceptionHandler`, `DuplicateIdentityException`, `InvalidPostcodeException`)

Mapper/DTO: `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/mapper/OwnerMapper.java`; DTOs are generated from `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/resources/openapi.yml` (owner schema at lines ~2090-2199, defining `locality`, `customerCode`, `membershipNumber`, `checkDigit`, `householdId`, `namesakeCount`, `membershipLevel`, `contactPreference`, `identityKey` as read-only).

Orchestration: `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/resources/officefloor/rest/api/owners.POST.yml`

Persistence columns (`customer_code`, `membership_number`, `household_id`, `namesake_count`, `household_size`, `membership_level`) appear in the DB schemas under `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/resources/db/{h2,hsqldb,mysql,postgres}/schema.sql`.

### Key inconsistencies to note for the redesign
- `AssignCustomerCode` javadoc example says `LON-SMI-0007` but `AssignMembershipNumber` javadoc example says `SMI-0007-M26` (missing the city segment) — the doc examples disagree.
- OpenAPI still documents the old city-only locality and the old `<CITY3>-<LAST3>-<NNNN>` customerCode; the acceptance test already expects the new `<REGION>-<HASH8>` form. `getLocality()` already does postcode-first while its OpenAPI text does not mention postcode.

